<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aq Qoyunlu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aq Qoyunlu, or 'White Sheep', were a Turkoman tribal confederation that ruled eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the second half of the fifteenth century. They expanded at the expense of Timurid territories in the west. The Safavid leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> defeated them in the early sixteenth century and took over their lands as the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu.jpg" alt="Aq Qoyunlu" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmadf.alabbasi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%88%D8%B2%D9%88%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%86.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isolated after the surrender of Sinop and the withdrawal of its Turkmen ally Uzun Hasan of the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b>, the city surrendered after a siege of about a month.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The last war was with the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b>: Jahan Shah's attempt to take Diyarbakır from them in 1466 ended in catastrophe, and he died at the Battle of Chapakchur in 1467.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…fourteen-year-old Ismail I, head of the Safaviyya Sufi order based at Ardabil in north-western Iran, led the Qizilbash, a confederation of Turkmen tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b> at the Battle of Sharur, seized Tabriz and proclaimed himself Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers were tributaries in turn of the Abbasid Caliphate, the Seljuk Empire, the Kingdom of Georgia, the Mongol Empire and the Ilkhanate, and later of the Timurids and the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b>, paying tribute and supplying troops while the dynasty endured at home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Turkmen confederations of the Kara Koyunlu and then the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b> absorbed the western Persian territories, and Abu Sa'id (r. 1451–1469), the last Timurid to reunite much of the realm, was defeated and killed in 1469 while campaigning against the Aq Qoyunlu leader…</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Erzincan in eastern Anatolia, Ottoman artillery and firearms gave Sultan Mehmed II victory over Uzun Hasan of the <b>Aq Qoyunlu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
